// The forward-pass guest<->host surface (submits PTIR container bytes). The WIT
// surface family is named `forward*`; PTIR remains the name of the container
// byte format it ships.
//
// Layering: the guest traces a pass -> canonical PTIR container bytes
// (pie_sampling_ir::ptir). WIT cannot carry closures, so the container bytes
// stay the wire artifact -- but everything the author manipulates (channels,
// working sets, the pass, the pipeline) crosses the boundary as a first-class
// resource, mirroring the ptir tracing DSL.
//
// THREE INTERFACES, ONE PER STATE SEMANTICS (forward_refactor.md D1):
//
//   `forward`            paged, per-token, reversibly discardable KV only
//   `forward-recurrent`  irreversibly folded recurrent state only
//   `forward-hybrid`     both, in the same forward
//
// The split is over STATE SEMANTICS, not model family: `architecture()` is an
// open, fast-growing string set, while this taxonomy is closed. `model.pass-kind()`
// selects the interface; constructing the wrong one errors immediately.
//
// The split is also a CORRECTNESS GATE. Attention-only algorithms (H2O, SnapKV,
// TOVA, Quest) are semantically wrong on a folded recurrent state -- evicting KV
// does not undo the fold -- and until now nothing caught it. They can no longer
// name a hybrid model's pass type.
//
// All three deliberately DUPLICATE `embed` / `readout` / `program` / `submit`
// and their geometry records rather than sharing a `pass-body` (D3): WIT
// duplication does not force implementation duplication (the host keeps one
// private `PassCore`), and full separation makes cross-kind states
// unrepresentable instead of runtime-guarded. Adding a field to one interface's
// record must never move another's ABI.

// THIS FILE: recurrent layers only, no attention layers anywhere in the model.
// Valid only when `model.pass-kind()` == recurrent.
//
// Legal stages: prologue, on_recurrent, epilogue. `on_attn_proj` / `on_attn`
// are REJECTED -- there is no attention layer for them to fire on, which under
// the old single-interface surface was an unexpressible (and unchecked) error.
//
// No registered model reports this kind today: Qwen3.5 GDN and Nemotron-H
// Mamba2 all interleave attention layers and so are `hybrid`. This interface
// exists so that a pure-SSM model is a typed case rather than a surprise.

interface forward-recurrent {
    use types.{error, data};
    use channel.{channel};
    use working-set.{rs-working-set, page-span};
    use pipeline.{pipeline};

    /// The recurrent counterpart of `kv-geometry`: the BUFFERED slots of the
    /// bound recurrent-state working sets. Every channel value is a
    /// WorkingSet-relative buffer page index; the runtime translates them to
    /// physical slots and lowers them as the `rs_buffer_slot_ids` /
    /// `rs_buffer_slot_indptr` wire fields.
    ///
    /// The FOLDED state slot is not named here -- it comes from the
    /// working-set handle itself.
    ///
    /// It is REQUIRED by `buffer` and `fold-buffered`, and does not appear on
    /// `attention` or `fold` at all. Hanging it off the state binding would
    /// have forced a plain `fold` prefill/decode -- which never touches the
    /// buffer -- to invent one, which is exactly the mandatory-dummy-geometry
    /// wart this refactor exists to remove. Attaching it to the two methods
    /// that actually read it makes it unconditional where it is meaningful and
    /// absent where it is not.
    record rs-geometry {
        /// Buffer page range a `fold-buffered` replay may read.
        readable-buffer: page-span,
        /// Buffer page range a `buffer` write may target.
        writable-buffer: page-span,
        /// Live buffered token count per request.
        buffer-len:    borrow<channel>,
        /// CSR values -- buffer page ids, densely packed per request.
        buffer-pages:  borrow<channel>,
        /// CSR row bounds, one entry per request plus one.
        buffer-indptr: borrow<channel>,
        /// Buffer page each token of this fire writes into.
        w-slot:        borrow<channel>,
        /// Offset within that page.
        w-off:         borrow<channel>,
    }

    resource forward-pass {
        constructor();

        /// State binding. REQUIRED. The mechanism is a recurrence, but the
        /// SLOT NAME is `attention` in all three interfaces (D6) -- the role
        /// inside a pass is the same regardless of mechanism; only the
        /// signature varies.
        ///
        /// `rs` is one working set per request, in resolved request order.
        /// The buffered geometry is NOT part of the state binding -- see
        /// `rs-geometry`.
        attention: func(rs: list<borrow<rs-working-set>>) -> result<_, error>;

        // -- Fold mode ------------------------------------------------
        // The former `rs-mode` variant, flattened into methods (D5): adding a
        // variant case is BREAKING, adding a resource method is ADDITIVE, and
        // `rs-mode` was the highest-evolution-risk type in the old surface.
        // At most one of the three; `fold` is the default if none is called.

        /// Fold every token of this fire into the folded state, in-forward and
        /// IRREVERSIBLY. The plain prefill/decode path and the default. Needs
        /// no buffer geometry -- it never touches the buffer.
        fold: func() -> result<_, error>;

        /// Write each token's pre-recurrence activations into the buffered
        /// slots named by `rs-geometry.w-slot` / `w-off`, leaving the folded
        /// state UNTOUCHED. This is what makes a linear model speculatable: an
        /// uncertain tail that is never folded costs nothing to abandon.
        /// Requires an already-materialized folded state.
        ///
        /// `start-token` is the buffer-relative token offset this fire writes
        /// from, and must be a multiple of `model.rs-buffer-page-size()`: the
        /// driver fills a request's slabs page-major FROM SLAB ZERO of the
        /// listed CSR span, so an unaligned start would silently shift every
        /// token within its slab. It is REDUNDANT with `rs-geometry.w-slot` /
        /// `w-off` and is ignored when those are bound -- it exists because
        /// the host-derived lowering path (which derives the buffered CSR from
        /// `(start-token, row-tokens)`) is the only one wired end to end today.
        buffer: func(start-token: u32, geom: rs-geometry) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Replay buffered tokens into the folded state: one length per bound
        /// working set, in request order. Runs ONLY the recurrent layers,
        /// advances the folded boundary, and drops fully covered head slots.
        /// Each length must be a positive multiple of
        /// `model.rs-fold-granularity()`.
        fold-buffered: func(lens: list<u32>, geom: rs-geometry) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Both `embed` and `readout` may be omitted under `fold-buffered`:
        /// that fire replays buffered tokens through the recurrent layers
        /// only, so nothing is embedded and no logits are produced.
        ///
        /// Bind embedding token ids and CSR row indptr. Both are channels.
        embed: func(
            tokens: borrow<channel>,
            indptr: borrow<channel>,
        ) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Bind an optional readout-index channel separately from embedding.
        readout: func(indices: borrow<channel>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Attach canonical PTIR bytes and channel handles in dense declaration
        /// order. Validation uses the engine-owned ModelProfile, and now also
        /// rejects any stage this interface does not admit -- see the per-file
        /// note on which stages are legal.
        program: func(
            container-bytes: data,
            channels: list<borrow<channel>>,
        ) -> result<_, error>;
    }

    /// Submit ONE FRAME on `on`: exactly `model.frame-size()` ordered slots.
    /// Slot i executes in wave i of the frame; the composer never moves a
    /// slot to another wave. `none` is a no-op for that wave. The slot list
    /// may repeat the same handle (a plain decode frame is the same pass in
    /// every slot) and may be heterogeneous (prefill chunks in the earliest
    /// slots, decode in the rest). A frame is one call, not a resource.
    ///
    /// Submission validation (deterministic, structural — never timing):
    /// - `slots` must hold exactly `frame-size()` entries with at least one
    ///   non-no-op slot.
    /// - Every host-writer channel bound by the frame's non-no-op slots must
    ///   be *staged* (it holds at least as many staged cells as the frame's
    ///   fires will consume), *device-advanced* (the program contains an
    ///   advance rule for it), or *latest-value* (the program only reads it —
    ///   a control word; one committed cell suffices and host `set` may
    ///   replace it at any time).
    /// - Per host-reader channel, occupancy plus cells reserved by accepted
    ///   unsettled frames plus the cells this frame will write must fit the
    ///   capacity — overflow is prevented here, never by back-pressure.
    ///
    /// Each non-no-op slot prepares and enqueues RUN-AHEAD in slot order,
    /// including passes that bind recurrent state: an RS mapping publishes at
    /// prepare, so slot i+1 sees slot i's decision without waiting for it to
    /// execute, and the state's contents are ordered by the stream. The first
    /// submit of a pass binds seeds; steady-state resubmits carry the
    /// identity hash + instance only. Staged host-writer puts coalesce into
    /// the slot fires in order.
    ///
    /// The result covers validation/preparation/enqueue only. A fire that
    /// fails AFTER submit poisons the pass's host-reader channels (surfaced
    /// at `take`/`read` with the fire's error) and fails the pass — further
    /// submits of it error with the root cause. End-of-stream is a PIPELINE
    /// event, not a fire attribute: call `pipeline.close` after the last
    /// submit.
    submit: func(
        on: borrow<pipeline>,
        slots: list<option<borrow<forward-pass>>>,
    ) -> result<_, error>;
}
